gned}|"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-130030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-130030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $4$th row of the matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{42}+1\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-130030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7adbcc7d52bf654d087d6f7659d0f66e0953a7b1d71173c43b6e23d57ef5d320"} {"question" "q-130030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-130030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|"] ["math" "-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|"] ["math" "2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|"] ["math" "2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|"] ["math" "3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-130030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using cofactor expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+1\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 5=15.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7adbcc7d52bf654d087d6f7659d0f66e0953a7b1d71173c43b6e23d57ef5d320"} {"question" "q-70108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "17"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(9-2) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=-4\\cdot (-14)=56.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7adbcc7d52bf654d087d6f7659d0f66e0953a7b1d71173c43b6e23d57ef5d320"} {"question" "q-70109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "38"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "56"] ["math" "62"] ["math" "72"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "56"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $4$th row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+3\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{21}+2\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-3)+2(1-3) \\\\  & =-5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-2)+(-2+1) \\\\  & =-2+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot (-9)+3\\cdot (-3)=18-9=9.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7adbcc7d52bf654d087d6f7659d0f66e0953a7b1d71173c43b6e23d57ef5d320"} {"question" "q-70133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-7"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $2$nd row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+1\\cdot {C}_{24} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{2+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+2\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(6-2)-(-6-2) \\\\  & =-8-(-8) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+1\\cdot {C}_{12}+2\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-6-2)+2(0-6) \\\\  & =-(-8)+2\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =8-12 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|=-0+(-4)=-4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/state.json" "file_sha256" "66957468364924e0d88f073f88579bb8126c0661e2a19cd104e310f958496971"} {"question" "q-70128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040832/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7adbcc7d52bf654d087d6f7659d0f66e0953a7b1d71173c43b6e23